{"chapter": "etale-cohomology", "node_kind": "theorem", "parent_tag": "03QW", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$S = \\Spec(K)$", "MATH_0002": "$K$", "MATH_0003": "$\\overline{s}$", "MATH_0004": "$S$", "MATH_0005": "$G = \\text{Gal}_{\\kappa(s)}$", "MATH_0006": "$$\n\\Sh(S_\\etale) \\longrightarrow G\\textit{-Sets},\n\\quad\n\\mathcal{F} \\longmapsto \\mathcal{F}_{\\overline{s}}.\n$$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{theorem}\n\\label{theorem-equivalence-sheaves-point}\n", "suffix": "\\end{theorem}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:826cf88935baac33e510fd0c760b3b78a49270724891ba9f6645509a2b654db2", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:88974591e72ec9e159b1bc008a7b2b032cf720000485008800598e575e3fe24b", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> with <MATH_0002> a field.\nLet <MATH_0003> be a geometric point of <MATH_0004>.\nLet <MATH_0005> denote the absolute Galois group.\nTaking stalks induces an equivalence of categories\n<MATH_0006>\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:fb1b84f5f6367a38f408e0ed89678557fa57fa97cb47a2690947d695f668a749", "unit_id": "tag:03QT:statement"}
{"chapter": "etale-cohomology", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "03QW", "placeholders": {"ACCENT_0001": "\\'e", "ACCENT_0002": "\\'e", "MATH_0001": "$G$", "MATH_0002": "$M$", "MATH_0003": "$X \\to \\Spec(K)$", "MATH_0004": "$\\Mor_K(\\Spec(K^{sep}), X)$", "MATH_0005": "$M$", "MATH_0006": "$G$", "MATH_0007": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0008": "$\\Spec(K)_\\etale$", "MATH_0009": "$U \\mapsto \\Mor_K(U, X)$", "MATH_0010": "$\\mathcal{F}_{\\overline{s}} = \\Mor_K(\\Spec(K^{sep}), X) = M$", "MATH_0011": "$G$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{pione-lemma-sheaves-point}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:edbf1f580c8410b133e59b5abbac7250bb6305d1d2411f235b75f0c2e9323200", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:be626e1bc0cec4309d0f868b947ec4c11805cfcb6b7fdd3005206cb0ac83d3f4", "source_text": "Let us construct the inverse to this functor. In\nFundamental Groups, Lemma <REF_0001>\nwe have seen that given a <MATH_0001>-set <MATH_0002> there exists an <ACCENT_0001>tale morphism\n<MATH_0003>\nsuch that <MATH_0004> is\nisomorphic to <MATH_0005> as a <MATH_0006>-set. Consider the sheaf\n<MATH_0007> on <MATH_0008> defined by\nthe rule <MATH_0009>. This is a sheaf as the <ACCENT_0002>tale\ntopology is subcanonical. Then we see that\n<MATH_0010>\nas <MATH_0011>-sets (details omitted). This gives the inverse of the functor and\nwe win.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:6a7cce4dbabc12496777cb2310b958ed36406548dba4a8be6ecd73f16bfe44b5", "unit_id": "tag:03QT:proof"}
{"chapter": "etale-cohomology", "node_kind": "remark", "parent_tag": "03QW", "placeholders": {"ACCENT_0001": "\\'e", "ACCENT_0002": "\\'e", "MATH_0001": "$\\Spec(K)_\\etale$", "MATH_0002": "$X$", "MATH_0003": "$\\Spec(K)$", "MATH_0004": "$\\Spec(K)_\\etale$", "MATH_0005": "$\\Spec(K)$", "MATH_0006": "$\\Spec(K)_\\etale$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{theorem-equivalence-sheaves-point}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{pione-lemma-sheaves-point}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{sites-definition-representable-sheaf}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{remark}\n\\label{remark-every-sheaf-representable}\n", "suffix": "\\end{remark}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:ff4484f9db6a69d8020f1ab62d01c8633ed85ff42f05e2be7a80da82864c7e1f", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:0faf2c36a36539cd57e311d43f24c551f777d5d768f7d3108cc41418cbcdc73b", "source_text": "Another way to state the conclusion of\nTheorem <REF_0001> and\nFundamental Groups, Lemma <REF_0002>\nis to say that every sheaf on <MATH_0001> is representable\nby a scheme <MATH_0002> <ACCENT_0001>tale over <MATH_0003>.\nThis does not mean that every sheaf is representable in the sense of\nSites, Definition <REF_0003>.\nThe reason is that in our construction of <MATH_0004>\nwe chose a sufficiently large set of schemes <ACCENT_0002>tale over <MATH_0005>,\nwhereas sheaves on <MATH_0006> form a proper class.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:d1612ca0ce318f3373c31788b159938b617c179141337b784aaa399dbb3f4f34", "unit_id": "tag:04JL:statement"}
